From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Refael Ackermann <refack@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 14:37:55 -0400
Subject: repository: make repo_clear() reset the flags that guard freed state

repo_clear() frees the repository state but leaves two booleans still claiming
it is there, so the second setup of the same struct repository walks into what
the first one freed:

  - ->initialized makes initialize_repository() BUG("repository initialized
    already").
  - ->worktree_initialized makes set_git_work_tree() strcmp() the new work
    tree against ->worktree, which repo_clear() has just FREE_AND_NULL()d, so
    it segfaults rather than reporting anything.

Both are the same defect: repo_clear() is meant to be the inverse of setting
the repository up, and undoes everything except the flags that say it happened.

Nothing upstream reaches a second setup, because git processes set up one
repository and exit, and 2.55 is the release that introduced both flags - which
is why they surface together now. TortoiseGit gitdll does reach it: it clears
and re-initializes the_repository when it is pointed at a different working
copy.

This one is not TortoiseGit-specific behaviour, only TortoiseGit-specific
exposure, and is worth sending to git upstream rather than carrying.
---
 repository.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/repository.c b/repository.c
index cfd00c3746..c07fc0cb71 100644
--- a/repository.c
+++ b/repository.c
@@ -430,6 +430,25 @@ void repo_clear(struct repository *repo)
 	strmap_clear(&repo->worktree_ref_stores, 1);
 
 	repo_clear_path_cache(&repo->cached_paths);
+
+	/*
+	 * repo_clear() is the inverse of setting the repository up, so it has to
+	 * undo these two flags as well. Both of them guard state that the code
+	 * above has just freed:
+	 *
+	 *  - ->initialized makes initialize_repository() BUG() a second time.
+	 *  - ->worktree_initialized makes set_git_work_tree() strcmp() against
+	 *    ->worktree, which FREE_AND_NULL'd above, so it segfaults.
+	 *
+	 * Upstream never notices either, because git processes set up one
+	 * repository and exit. gitdll re-points a long-lived process at a
+	 * different repository (see reset_git_env(), which clears and then
+	 * re-initializes), so it is the only caller that ever reaches a second
+	 * setup. ->worktree_initialized is where v2.55 moved the file-scope
+	 * git_work_tree_initialized that reset_setup() used to clear.
+	 */
+	repo->initialized = false;
+	repo->worktree_initialized = false;
 }
 
 int repo_read_index(struct repository *repo)
